Single-database Alembic configuration for Flask-Migrate.

Fresh development/test database:

    flask --app backend.app:create_app db upgrade

Existing legacy SQLite database:

The existing database is not modified automatically by this repository change. Before applying any migration:

1. Stop the application and make a separate backup copy of `cinezone.db`.
2. Verify the backup and confirm the source database passes `PRAGMA integrity_check`.
3. Confirm the legacy tables match the original schema represented by revision `55b61e8704a3`.
4. Point `DATABASE_URL` explicitly at the existing database and stamp only the baseline revision. Stamping records migration history; it does not execute the baseline DDL.
5. Apply the additive migration and verify the resulting revision and schema.

Example (replace the database path with the verified absolute path):

    APP_ENV=development DATABASE_URL=sqlite:////absolute/path/cinezone.db flask --app backend.app:create_app db stamp 55b61e8704a3
    APP_ENV=development DATABASE_URL=sqlite:////absolute/path/cinezone.db flask --app backend.app:create_app db upgrade
    APP_ENV=development DATABASE_URL=sqlite:////absolute/path/cinezone.db flask --app backend.app:create_app db current
    APP_ENV=development DATABASE_URL=sqlite:////absolute/path/cinezone.db flask --app backend.app:create_app db check

Do not stamp an unverified schema, run these commands against the only copy, or downgrade a production database. The additive revisions create the rights, asset, details, view, and administrator audit tables; if development schema initialization already created them, the revisions skip those existing tables.

The legacy SQLite-to-MySQL importer is separate: create and migrate an empty destination database, review a verified source backup, then run `flask --app backend.app:create_app import-legacy-sqlite /path/to/backup.db`. The importer reads the source read-only and refuses a non-empty destination.
